    feat(cli): optional bubblewrap backend with private /proc, unshare fallback. Addresses TJ-GAP-054.
    
    standalone/terminal-jail learns a runtime-detected backend selector:
    TERMINAL_JAIL_JAIL_BACKEND=auto|bwrap|unshare (default auto, no new CLI flags).
    auto uses bwrap when it is installed AND its namespace probe passes, else the
    unchanged unshare backend; bwrap is a demand that fails closed (exit 2, command
    not run) when missing or unusable — never a silent downgrade; unshare pins the
    pre-v1.2 behavior byte-for-byte; an unknown value exits 2 before any namespace
    work.
    
    bwrap invocation (all measurements on bubblewrap 0.11.1, unprivileged):
      bwrap --unshare-user --unshare-pid --die-with-parent --bind / / \
            --dev-bind /dev /dev --proc /proc -- bash -c 'exec "$@"' terminal-jail ...
    - --proc /proc mounts a fresh procfs: private /proc (jail sees 5 PIDs vs 1682
      host PIDs; /proc/1 is bwrap's reaper, not the host init) — the gap this
      backend exists to close, including for --user where unshare exposes the host
      /proc.
    - --die-with-parent (PR_SET_PDEATHSIG) tears the sandbox down on a SIGKILL of
      the wrapper; the control run without the flag is orphaned.
    - --dev-bind /dev /dev is load-bearing: with --bind / / alone, /dev/null,
      /dev/zero and /dev/urandom return EACCES inside the sandbox, which broke the
      --seccomp loader's Python interpreter (venv PATH) hash randomization.
    - --as-pid-1 is deliberately NOT used: measured, it nullifies
      --die-with-parent (orphaned jail), so the payload runs as PID 2 behind
      bubblewrap's reaper.
    - No added filesystem isolation: host root and host /dev are bound in exactly
      as the unshare backend inherits them.
    
    Preserved: the unshare launch form and its exit/error semantics, --user mapped
    filesystem isolation (auto keeps unshare there because bubblewrap has no
    unprivileged --map-users equivalent — measured: --uid alone leaves a
    caller-owned mode-600 file readable; bwrap --user states the loss loudly and
    reports TERMINAL_JAIL_FS_ISOLATION=degraded), --seccomp, interruptor
    block/modify/warn behavior, the argv trampoline, and the fd/PTY contract.
    
    Tests: plugin/test_backend_selection.py (16 cases — selection, the documented
    flag contract, per-backend argv preservation with metacharacter/empty args,
    bwrap-absent fallback, explicit-bwrap missing binary, explicit-bwrap probe
    failure, auto probe-failure degradation, the --user mapping rule, and live
    private-/proc + exit-semantics checks that skip with HOST-DEGRADED-BWRAP);
    plugin/test_install.py's unshare-failure test now pins
    TERMINAL_JAIL_JAIL_BACKEND=unshare so it keeps asserting the same contract.
    Full suite: 561 passed / 5 pre-existing skips (baseline 536/14 — nine bare-mode
    tests previously skipped as HOST-DEGRADED-PIDNS now run for real via bwrap on
    this host). ruff clean; gitreins guard PASS.
    
    Docs: specs/cli.md (usage block now byte-matches the shipped --help, new
    section 4 "Jail backends" with the selection policy, per-backend property
    table and honest limits; sections 1, 6, 7, 8-adjacent error/status tables, 9
    test matrix CLI-17..CLI-25, 10 acceptance), README (How It Works, Components,
    Graceful Degradation, Requirements, Host Limitations), docs/quickstart.md
    (backend selection + FAQ), docs/deploy-to-karahermes.md, CHANGELOG.
    
    Host-degraded limitation (unchanged and documented): this host denies the
    --user uid mapping (AppArmor unprivileged_userns), so there is no mapped
    filesystem isolation to compare against; bwrap + --user correctly reports
    TERMINAL_JAIL_FS_ISOLATION=degraded.
    
    Addresses TJ-GAP-054.
    
